      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's crucial to present a balanced educational viewpoint. Consider blending the historical context with the current narrative.\n\nLesson 2: Systemic Racism in the Housing Market - Addressing Counter-Arguments\n\n- Acknowledge the common counter-argument by introducing the notion that some people believe the wealth gap is more a consequence of individual choices or economic changes. Ask why they believe this to be the case.\n\n- Present this belief as one of the many perspectives, ensuring students understand that historical context provides a foundation of how our society has evolved, valuewise, financially, and socially.\n\n- Compare and contrast the anti-systemic racism views by showing the limitations of individual choice within systematic constraints—like how it’s challenging to reverse wealth disparities when opportunities are historically skewed.\n\n- Introduce social science research, including studies from economists and sociologists, that supports the systemic roots of the wealth gap due to discriminatory practices, while referring to some \"contrarian\" research.\n\n- Project the different findings onto the concept of \"human agency.\" Highlight how individuals are indeed empowered within a given context. In the shadow of historical discrimination, where choices are limited by external barriers, the scope of agency is significantly narrowed for affected communities.\n\n- Use cases where a conscious effort to overcome one's",
